{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"marshy-chummy-coot","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Why this exists\n\n`irongrey-chubby-oxpecker` (`aa7b-5e47-6d3d-4615`) is the bug: the Jira reconcile bridge's\nPRIMARY comment-dedup skip — `binding_store.is_comment_mapped(local_comment_key)`, keyed on\ncomment ID — was **inert in production**, because the `comment_ids` map in\n`.bridge_state/bindings.json` is empty:\n\n```\nbindings:     2475 entries\ncomment_ids:     0 entries\n```\n\nThis ticket is not that bug. It is the question the bug raises: **how did a silently-inert\nprimary guard survive review, tests, CI and months of production, and what would have caught\nit?**\n\n## Why it is worth asking here specifically\n\nThree properties make this a high-value escape to study, not a routine one:\n\n1. **It was silent by construction.** `_record_comment_id`\n   (`dispatch_apply_phases.py:309-334`) returns quietly on any of four guard conditions. Nothing\n   logged, nothing raised, no metric moved. The system had no way to tell an operator that its\n   primary dedup key had never been populated.\n2. **A working fallback hid it.** The SECONDARY body-equality skip — described in-code as\n   belt-and-suspenders for \"a lost map write\" — absorbed enough traffic that the failure looked\n   like partial success rather than total failure of the primary path. Short comments deduped\n   (REB-424: 22/25); only richly-formatted ones did not (REB-1861: 1/40). A degradation that\n   correlates with content shape is exactly the kind that reads as flakiness.\n3. **A prior fix in the same area did not surface it.** `florid-vigorous-urial`\n   (`1f3d-cb66-d3fc-4356`) fixed 5000-cap comment inflation via pagination, and that fix is\n   present and correct. It **hardened the fallback's inputs while the primary skip was already\n   inert** — so the area had recent, competent attention that did not reveal the deeper problem.\n   That is the most interesting fact here.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis is an **escape analysis**, in the manner of `cranial-goodly-seahog`. It should produce\nfindings and, where warranted, concrete detection proposals — NOT a re-fix of the bridge, which\n`aa7b` owns.\n\nQuestions to answer with evidence:\n\n- **Detection.** What signal existed that a reasonable operator or test could have read? Was\n  `comment_ids` ever non-empty — i.e. did this regress, or was it never populated? Git history on\n  `.bridge_state/bindings.json` should settle that, and the answer changes the analysis\n  completely.\n- **Test gap.** Do the reconciler's tests exercise the primary ID skip at all, or do they pass a\n  binding store that makes the fallback sufficient? A test that would still pass with\n  `is_comment_mapped` hard-wired to `False` cannot detect this class.\n- **Review gap.** Would any current plan-review or code-review criterion have flagged a\n  silently-returning guard on a correctness-critical path? If not, is that a criterion worth\n  proposing — bearing in mind the project's rule that a capability whose only trigger is a\n  specific CI system is not portable (`project.portability`).\n- **Observability gap.** Should a dedup map that is empty while its sibling map holds thousands\n  of entries be an alertable invariant? This is the cheapest candidate remedy and should be\n  evaluated on its merits rather than assumed.\n- **Fallback design.** The general pattern — a secondary guard silently carrying a failed\n  primary — is likely not unique to this file. Is it worth a targeted sweep?\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Established from evidence (git history of `.bridge_state/bindings.json`) whether\n      `comment_ids` was ever populated, so the escape is correctly classified as a regression\n      versus never-worked.\n- [ ] The test gap is characterised: named tests that cover the dedup path, and a demonstration\n      of whether they would still pass with the primary skip disabled.\n- [ ] The review gap is characterised: whether any existing plan-review or code-review criterion\n      addresses silently-inert guards, with the criterion ids checked.\n- [ ] At least one concrete, portable detection proposal is made — or a reasoned argument that\n      none is worth its cost, which is an acceptable outcome if argued.\n- [ ] Findings are recorded on this ticket; any proposed criterion or test work is filed as its\n      own ticket rather than implemented here.\n\n## Sequencing\n\nBlocked by `aa7b-5e47-6d3d-4615`: the escape cannot be analysed accurately until the mechanism is\nconfirmed by that ticket's root-cause pass. Its finding of WHICH of the four silent guards fires\nis a direct input here.","id":"2399-5e49-6b28-4d59","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Escape analysis: how a silently-inert primary dedup guard survived review, tests and months of production"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787252832617338001,"uuid":"313a4a2b-ddce-490b-80d7-1264a6809392"}